<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Patna State</h1><p class="st-meta">1360 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Bolangir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chauhan Rajput state in western Odisha, once head of a cluster of eighteen hill forts, whose last Maharaja became Chief Minister of Orissa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramai-deva">Ramai Deva</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan Rajput</a>, founded the dynasty at Patnagarh in the mid-14th century and became overlord of the surrounding chiefs, a group known as the Athara Garhjat, or eighteen forts. A branch of the family later founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sambalpur-state">Sambalpur State</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> was a large inland state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields and forest, home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondh">Kondh</a>, Binjhal and other communities. Its capital later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balangir">Bolangir</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> it was tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagpur-kingdom">Nagpur Kingdom</a>, and under the British it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orissa-tributary-states">Orissa Tributary States</a> from 1905.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> on 1 January 1948. Its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-narayan-singh-deo">Rajendra Narayan Singh Deo</a>, entered politics and served as Chief Minister of Orissa from 1967 to 1971. The area is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balangir">Bolangir</a> district.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1360 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.77%;width:11.70%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1360 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bolangir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ramai Deva, Rajendra Narayan Singh Deo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sambalpuri, Odia</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Patnagarh, Bolangir-Patna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1654&amp;focus=patna-state" aria-label="Patna State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6927 1411 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Patna State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7247 1667L7364 1601M7247 1667L7364 1602M7247 1667L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7247 1667h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7247 1667h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1654&amp;focus=patna-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
